# Spectra — Screens, the HUD, and audio

This file defines the seven screens the game moves between, what each shows, the HUD
readouts, and the audio cues. The keys the screens are driven by are in
`specs/controls.md`, and the run the `inWave` screen carries is in
`specs/progression.md`. Every piece of screen copy below carries the name this
specification gives it.

Every piece of text a screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it at the
logical stage size, `1280 x 720`. The palette, the type, and the layout of each
screen are yours.

## The screens

The game is on exactly one of seven screens at a time.

| Screen | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | The main menu, and where the game opens. |
| `howto` | How to play. |
| `stageIntro` | The hold that announces the stage about to be played. |
| `inWave` | Live play, in the two phases `specs/progression.md` states. |
| `paused` | The pause menu, over the frozen field. |
| `stageCleared` | The interstitial between a finished stage and the next. |
| `gameOver` | The run was lost. |

### `title`

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `SPECTRA` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `TUNE TO SURVIVE` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | The mode entry `specs/mode.md` names, then `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's items are stacked one above the next under the title and the tagline. The
highlighted item is the one the menu index names, counted from `0`, and it is drawn
distinctly from the others, so a player always sees which item `confirm` would take.
The highlight rests on the first item when the game opens at the title. Every later
arrival at the title puts the highlight on the title entry that led away from it:
`HOW TO PLAY` after the how-to-play screen, and the mode entry after a run, since a
run is opened from that entry. `confirm` takes the highlighted item:

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| The mode entry | Opens a new run and moves to `stageIntro` at stage `1`, with `START_LIVES` lives and a score of `0`. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Moves to `howto`. |

A dim slice of the starfield may show behind the menu if that suits the look you
design.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as rules of a system. It
covers:

- the goal, which is to clear each wave of drones before the last life is lost;
- that the ship holds one band at a time, that only a matching shot destroys a
  drone, and that the same band is the hull's shield;
- that the flip costs a beat of fire;
- the three drones and what each asks of the band system;
- the resonance meter and the discharge it pays for;
{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}
- what a wrong-band shot does to the drone it hits, as `specs/mode.md` states;
{{/if}}
- the controls.

The controls it names include the movement keys, written as the standalone words
`ARROWS` and `AD`, and the fire key, written as the standalone word `SPACE`. It
names both bands by their labels from `BAND_LABELS`, `CYAN` and `MAGENTA`.

`back` returns to `title`, with the title's highlight on `HOW TO PLAY`.

### `stageIntro`

A hold before the wave, over the field. It draws `HUD_STAGE_LABEL` (`STAGE`) and the
stage's number; on a challenge stage it draws `CHALLENGE_BANNER`
(`CHALLENGING STAGE`) as well.

It lasts `STAGE_INTRO_HOLD` (`2.0`) seconds and then opens the live wave, at which
moment the stage's wave is built as `specs/stages.md` states.

### `inWave`

The live field, carrying everything `specs/overview.md` lists, with the HUD strips
above and below. It runs in the `live` and `ready` phases. During the `ready` phase
it draws `READY_TEXT` (`READY`) over the field.

### `paused`

Reached by `pause` during live play. The field stays visible behind the menu and is
frozen: no drone moves, no bullet travels, no phase timer runs, none of the clocks
the wave keeps advances, and no cue plays, so a paused game is exactly where it was
when it was paused.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Menu | `PAUSE_ITEMS` | `RESUME`, `RESTART`, `QUIT TO MENU`, in that order |

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `RESUME` | Returns to `inWave` with the field and the run exactly as they were. |
| `RESTART` | Opens a new run, at stage `1` with `START_LIVES` lives and a score of `0`, and moves to `stageIntro`. |
| `QUIT TO MENU` | Returns to `title`, with the title's highlight on the mode entry. |

### `stageCleared`

The interstitial a finished stage opens. It lasts `STAGE_CLEARED_HOLD` (`2.6`)
seconds and then opens the next stage's intro, with the stage number one higher.

| After | It reports |
| --- | --- |
| A standard stage | That the stage was cleared, and the `SCORE_STAGE_CLEAR` bonus paid |
| A challenge stage in which every drone was destroyed | `PERFECT_TEXT` (`PERFECT!`) |
| A challenge stage in which a drone survived | The count of drones destroyed |

### `gameOver`

Opened when the last life is lost. It draws the run's final score and the stage it
reached.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Menu | `GAME_OVER_ITEMS` | `PLAY AGAIN`, then `MENU` |

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `PLAY AGAIN` | Opens a new run, at stage `1` with `START_LIVES` lives and a score of `0`, and moves to `stageIntro`. |
| `MENU` | Returns to `title`, with the title's highlight on the mode entry. |

## Pointer and touch

The three menu screens, `title`, `paused`, and `gameOver`, are driven by a mouse and
by touch as well as by the keyboard, over the items that screen shows. Each item
occupies a rectangular hit region, laid out with the menu itself, and
`specs/instrumentation.md` states the reading the build reports an item's region
through.

| Input | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| A pointer moves onto an item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index. |
| A touch contact lands inside an item's region, or travels onto one | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index. |
| A pointer is pressed and released inside one item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index, and that item is confirmed. |
| A touch contact lands and lifts inside one item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index, and that item is confirmed. |

A confirm takes both of its edges inside one item's region: the press and its release
for a pointer, the landing and the lift for a touch contact. Two edges that fall in
different regions, and an edge that falls outside every region, confirm no item. The
item every confirm acts on is the item at `menuIndex`, whichever input raised it, and
the effect is the one the screen's own table above gives that item.

The pointer and the touch contacts are read once per frame, in the same input read as
the keyboard actions, and are applied after that frame's keyboard edges. A press and
the release that follows it may arrive on one frame, and that frame confirms. A frame
carrying a keyboard movement edge together with a pointer or touch selection leaves
`menuIndex` at the item the pointer or the contact named, and a frame carrying a
keyboard `confirm` edge together with a pointer or touch confirm confirms the
keyboard's item alone.

A pointer and a touch contact drive the menus directly rather than through one of the
actions `specs/controls.md` names. The `howto`, `stageIntro`, `inWave`, and
`stageCleared` screens show no menu, and neither a pointer nor a contact does
anything on them.

## The HUD

The two HUD strips carry five readouts, each inside the strip `specs/field.md` puts
it in.

| Readout | Shows |
| --- | --- |
| Score | The running score, as digits. It is the most prominent readout. |
| Stage | `HUD_STAGE_LABEL` (`STAGE`) and the current stage's digits. |
| Lives | The lives remaining, as a row of icons or as a count. It changes as the lives change. |
| Resonance meter | The meter, as a bar whose filled extent grows with it from empty at `0` to full at `RESONANCE_MAX`. A full meter is drawn distinctly from one a point below full, so a player sees that a discharge is ready. |
| Polarity indicator | The ship's current band, in that band's color and shape accent, with its label from `BAND_LABELS` (`CYAN`, `MAGENTA`). It follows a flip in the frame the flip happens, and it always agrees with the band drawn on the ship. |

The bottom strip also carries a mute indicator. It is drawn whenever sound is muted
and is absent whenever it is not, and it is the only thing about the HUD or the
field that mute changes.

While a spectral inversion is active the play field carries a field-wide mark that
is absent otherwise, so a player sees at a glance that the bands are swapped.

Nothing else is required on either strip, and a further readout of your own is
welcome there.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write, synthesizing its sounds with the
Web Audio API rather than loading audio files. Define and play the nine cues below,
under exactly these names, one per event.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus. Define and play the nine cues below, under
the names `CUES` gives them, one per event.
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus: define the nine cues below from the game
instance's `initialize`, under the names `CUES` gives them, and play them from the
world's audio inside a tick, one per event.
{{/if}}

| Cue | Played when |
| --- | --- |
| `fire` | The ship fires a shot. |
| `flip` | The ship flips its band. |
| `absorb` | The hull absorbs an enemy bullet of its own band. |
| `kill` | A matching shot destroys a drone or a Prism's layer. |
| `discharge` | A discharge is released. |
| `inversion` | A spectral inversion begins. |
| `hit` | A life is lost. |
| `stage-clear` | A stage clears. |
| `menu` | A menu highlight moves. |

`specs/mode.md` states whether the mode this build ships plays a cue of its own
beside these.

Each is a distinct short sound, so the events are told apart by ear. Each is played
on the frame its event happens and at most once on that frame; a frame that raises
more than one of them plays each of those once.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Muting is yours as well, and the `mute` action toggles it from any screen. While
sound is muted the game starts no sound at all: a muted cue is not played and then
silenced, it is not played, so no source is started and no node is scheduled for it.
The game stays fully playable muted.

No sound is started between the page loading and the player's first key press: the
game plays no cue before that first input.
{{else}}
Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the engine, and the `mute` action
toggles the engine's mute bit from any screen. While sound is muted the game starts
no sound at all: it plays no cue rather than playing one for the bus to silence, so
no cue reaches the bus while muted. The game stays fully playable muted.

No sound is started between the page loading and the player's first key press: the
game plays no cue before that first input.
{{/if}}

## Out of scope

- Network or online play.
- Gamepad input.
- Persistence of progress, scores, or settings between sessions. Each session starts
  fresh.
- Capturing or turning a drone, an escort ship, or a weapon upgrade. The ship's one
  weapon is the band-carrying shot.
